<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Intaphrenes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 520 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a> · Also known as Intaphernes, Vindafarnâ, Vidafarnâ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Intaphrenes was a Persian, one of the seven who in September 522 BCE helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a> seize the throne of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a>. He then became Darius's bow carrier, a high position in which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a> shows him. In 521 BCE Darius sent him at the head of an army against Arakha, who had claimed the throne of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> IV; Babylon was taken without much fighting on 27 November, and Arakha and his chief followers were impaled. In the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, Intaphrenes was later put to death after he cut off the ears and noses of two officers who barred his way to the king, and Darius, fearing a revolt, killed his family except his wife's brother and son.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/intaphernes.jpg" alt="Intaphrenes" width="330" height="510" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leen van Dorp · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Intaphrenes_on_the_Behistun_Relief.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Darius's own account of the Babylonian campaign survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription">Behistun Inscription</a>. In it the king says that he sent an army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and appointed &quot;a Persian named Intaphrenes, my servant&quot; as its leader, with the order to smite the Babylonian host that did not acknowledge him. By the grace of Ahuramazda, the inscription continues, Intaphrenes overthrew the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> and brought the people over to Darius.</p><p>The inscription dates the capture of Arakha, who called himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a>, and of the men who were his chief followers to the twenty-second day of the month Markâsanaš. Darius then decreed that Arakha and those men be crucified in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. The punishment followed the custom in such cases: Arakha was mutilated and impaled together with his followers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Intaphrenes was put to death after the revolt had been put down. The seven noblemen who had toppled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardiya">Bardiya</a> had agreed that each of them might visit the new king whenever he pleased, except when the king was with his wife. One evening Intaphrenes came to the palace and was stopped by two officers, who told him that Darius had retired for the night. Enraged at the insult, he drew his sword, cut off their ears and noses, and tied the two men together with the bridle of his horse.</p><p>When the officers showed the king what had been done to them, Darius feared that all seven nobles had joined in a revolt. He sent to each of the others to ask whether they approved, and all disowned the act and declared that they stood by their choice of Darius as King of Kings. Darius then had Intaphrenes seized with his son, his relatives and any friends able to bear arms, although no proof of a planned rebellion was found when they were brought to court.</p><p>All were condemned, but Intaphrenes's wife was allowed to choose one member of her family to be saved. She chose her brother, reasoning that she could have another husband and another son but would only ever have one brother. Impressed by her answer, Darius spared both her brother and her son. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> does not mention the name of Intaphrenes again after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Intaphrenes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Six of them descended from the nobles who joined Darius in 522 BCE to kill the man he called the usurper Gaumata: Otanes, <b>Intaphernes</b>, Gobryas, Hydarnes, Megabyzus and Aspathines.</blockquote><a href="/societies/achaemenid-persia">Social Hierarchy of the Achaemenid Empire</a></li></ul></section>
